<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yusuf Adil Shah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1510">1510</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Yusuf Adil Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yusuf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shah">Adil Shah</a> was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty">Adil Shahi dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>. A governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a>, he declared independence around 1490 as Bahmani power broke apart. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> and neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> sultans, and lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> to the Portuguese under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> in 1510, the year of his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yusuf-adil-shah.jpg" alt="Yusuf Adil Shah" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kamal Muhammad; Chand Muhammad · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_House_of_Bijapur_Album_leaf,_ca._1680_Deccan,_Bijapur._Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yusuf was born in 1450, and accounts of his origin differ. He may have been a Georgian slave purchased by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-gawan">Mahmud Gawan</a>, the Bahmani prime minister; other historians call him Persian or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a>, and one theory makes him a Turkman of the Aq-Quyunlu. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firishta">Firishta</a> presents him as a son of the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a>, a story that modern historians consider unfounded. The Portuguese knew him as Hidalcão.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He rose quickly in the Bahmani sultan's favour and was made governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulatabad">Daulatabad</a>; Mahmood Shah Bahmani II gave him the title Adil Khan. He led the foreigners, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afaqis">Afaqis</a>, in their conflict with the local nobility, the Deccanis, a struggle in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-gawan">Mahmud Gawan</a> was put to death in 1481. Yusuf then left Daulatabad to seize Gawan's former territories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belagavi">Belgaum</a>, and soon came into conflict with Malik Naib, leader of the Deccani party.</p><p>He and his son Ismail did not place the title Shah before their names but kept Adil Khan, out of respect for the Bahmanis; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-adil-shah-i">Ibrahim Adil Shah I</a> formally claimed independence in 1538 and was the first of the line to use it. At Bijapur Yusuf built the citadel, the Arkilla, and the palace called the Faroukh Mahal. He invited poets and artisans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to his court and was himself an accomplished musician and scholar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He was killed in the Battle of Koilkonda against Vijayanagara on 5 December 1510, a few days after the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-adil-shah">Ismail Adil Shah</a>, a minor, succeeded him, aided in his rule by a certain Kamal Khan. Yusuf had married Punji, sister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> lord Mukundrao Kadam, who was renamed Bubuji Khanum; their daughters married the sultans of Ahmednagar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a> and a Bahmani prince. The sultanate he founded lasted for close to two centuries, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> ended it in 1686.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yusuf Adil Shah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1490 three of them effectively declared independence: <b>Yusuf Adil Khan</b> at Bijapur, Ahmad Nizam ul-Mulk at Ahmadnagar and Fathullah Imad ul-Mulk in Berar, founding the Adil Shahi, Nizam Shahi and Imad Shahi dynasties.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Bahmani sultans lost control to their great nobles in the late fifteenth century, <b>Yusuf Adil Khan</b>, governor of the province of Bijapur, made himself effectively independent around 1489–1490, while at first still acknowledging the Bahmani ruler.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Saveh, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in Shahpur, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.02,50.36&amp;year=1500" aria-label="Saveh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5826 897 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yusuf Adil Shah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6276 1167h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6276 1167h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.02,50.36&amp;year=1500">What was here in 1500? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
